                "text": "**Analyzing the Training Data**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the training data. The inputs are 10x10 grids with scattered colors. The goal? Creating mostly red pixels in a 3x3 square. It seems that train 0, has the 3x3 square at row 2..4.\n\n\n**Identifying the Correlation**\n\nI've been re-examining the training data, and I'm seeing a pattern! The input value at the center of the target 3x3 square seems to be the critical link. For instance, in several training examples, the corresponding input value at the center position matches the output value. But, I am stuck on how the 2 in the output might be driven by input with no 2!\n\n\n**Investigating the Data Relationships**\n\nI'm now diving deep into why the output values relate to the inputs. Specifically, I'm trying to figure out how a '6' in train 0's output relates to input values, given that it only has one '6' and NO '2' in the output. I've started examining other values to see if there's a pattern, and am counting occurrences. I am looking more closely at train data 0.\n\n\n**Uncovering the Core Pattern**\n\nI've made a breakthrough. I've realized the output's 3x3 square is a \"frame.\" Looking at train examples 1 and 2, when the center pixel is 2, the 3x3 square becomes entirely 2. Then, in train 0, I noticed the output 3x3 is 6, and the pixels surrounding (3,1) are also 2. I think the number 2 is acting as a \"frame\" color. Is 2 red?\n\n\n**Pinpointing the Center**\n\nOkay, I've got a better handle on this, now. It seems that the color that appears only once in a training example dictates the center of the 3x3 square. I saw that in train 1, there was only one '2', at (3,3)! In train 0, the single 6 pixel was also at the center of the output 3x3 frame!\n\n\n**Determining the Output**\n\nI've been analyzing the test input now. I'm focusing on the single-occurrence colors in each input. For train example 0, '6' appears only once. For the test input, I'm checking each color's frequency. I need to establish if this pattern holds. I will check for the unique occurrences.\n\n\n**Confirming the Frequency**\n\nI've just finished running the frequency analysis on the test data. Color '9' has a count of one! Its position is (6,5). I'm testing the 3x3 square pattern. Now I am trying to use the 3x3 square.\n\n\n",
